[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     45[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** `Scanner::scan_ulong` modelled `strtoul` as "clamp the magnitude,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     46[0m:[38;5;10m[49m then apply the sign". glibc does the opposite: as soon as the digit string
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     47[0m:[38;5;10m[49m overflows it sets `ERANGE` and returns `ULONG_MAX` *without* applying the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     48[0m:[38;5;10m[49m leading `-`. Only values that actually fit get negated. So `-1` → `ULONG_MAX`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     49[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (negation of 1) but `-99999999999999999999999999` → `ULONG_MAX` as well
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     50[0m:[38;5;10m[49m (saturation, no negation), whereas the old code computed
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     51[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `0 - ULONG_MAX == 1`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     52[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     53[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `translation/src/main.rs`, `Scanner::scan_ulong`: return `u64::MAX`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     54[0m:[38;5;10m[49m immediately when the digit accumulation overflowed, and only wrap the sign in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     55[0m:[38;5;10m[49m for magnitudes that fit. `%d` was already correct — glibc's signed path
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     56[0m:[38;5;10m[49m saturates to `LONG_MIN` / `LONG_MAX` and *is* sign-aware, and the port then
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     57[0m:[38;5;10m[49m truncates to `int`, which is why `99999999999999999999` reads back as `-1`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     58[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     59[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Regression cover.** `main_scanf_error_paths::err_input_len_overflow_ulong`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     60[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `err_ref_len_overflow_ulong`, `main_scalar_edge_cases::scan_flags_overflow_ulong`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     61[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `scan_op_neg_long_saturates`, plus `scanf_integer_semantics` in `src/main.rs`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     62[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     63[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Mismatch 2 — stdin was consumed eagerly instead of on demand
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     64[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     65[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Symptom.** Not visible in stdout/stderr/exit status for a finite pipe, but the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     66[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Rust program blocked where the C program did not: `scanf` stops reading the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     67[0m:[38;5;10m[49m moment its conversions are satisfied, so the C `driver` answers immediately even
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     68[0m:[38;5;10m[49m if the writer keeps the pipe open, while the Rust `driver` sat in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     69[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `read_to_end` waiting for EOF.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     70[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     71[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Cause.** `Scanner::new` called `stdin().read_to_end()` up front.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     72[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     73[0m:[38;5;10m[49m **Fix.** `Scanner` now buffers stdin incrementally (`Scanner::fill`, 4 KiB at a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     74[0m:[38;5;10m[49m time) and only pulls more bytes when the next byte of lookahead is not already
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     75[0m:[38;5;10m[49m buffered. Verified by holding the pipe open after a complete input: both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     76[0m:[38;5;10m[49m binaries print the result and exit at the same point.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     77[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     78[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Inputs where the C program is not reproducible (excluded, by design)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     79[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     80[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `main()` declares `char input_buffer[1024]` and `char ref_buffer[1024]` and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     81[0m:[38;5;10m[49m fills only the first `input_len` / `ref_len` bytes. `lib.c` then calls
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     82[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `strcmp`, `strlen` and `strncmp` on them — the comments in the C source flag
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     83[0m:[38;5;10m[49m these as `VULNERABLE`. When the declared length contains **no NUL byte**, those
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     84[0m:[38;5;10m[49m calls read pre-`main` stack residue left by the dynamic loader and libc
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     85[0m:[38;5;10m[49m start-up. Part of that residue is saved pointers, whose bytes change with ASLR,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     86[0m:[38;5;10m[49m so **the C binary returns different answers on different runs of the same
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     87[0m:[38;5;10m[49m input**. Measured on the reference build:
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     88[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     89[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | stdin | C results over 200 runs |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     90[0m:[38;5;10m[49m |---|---|
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     91[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `0 0 1 0 0` (op 0, `input=""`, `ref_len=0`) | `0` ×183, `1` ×17 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     92[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4 0 6 80 65 85 83 69 0 0` (op 4, `"PAUSE"`, `ref_len=0`) | `0` ×186, `5` ×14 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     93[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4 3 64 <63×'A'> 0 0` | `0` ×190, `10` ×10 |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     94[0m:[38;5;10m[49m | `4 2 0 0` | mostly SIGSEGV, sometimes `0` or `10` |
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     95[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     96[0m:[38;5;10m[49m No translation can be byte-identical to a program that is not byte-identical to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     97[0m:[38;5;10m[49m itself, so these inputs are deliberately **not** asserted on. The rule used to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     98[0m:[38;5;10m[49m classify a case is mechanical: an input is reproducible iff every buffer the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+     99[0m:[38;5;10m[49m operation dereferences as a string contains a NUL byte within its declared
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    100[0m:[38;5;10m[49m length. All 164 cases in `tests/differential.rs` satisfy that rule and were each
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    101[0m:[38;5;10m[49m confirmed stable over 30 consecutive runs of the C binary before being admitted.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    102[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    103[0m:[38;5;10m[49m `src/stack_residue.rs` models the residue so that the port still behaves
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    104[0m:[38;5;10m[49m plausibly on these inputs — it reproduces the modal C answer in the cases
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    105[0m:[38;5;10m[49m measured above — but its ASLR-dependent bytes cannot be matched exactly, and
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    106[0m:[38;5;10m[49m nothing in the test suite depends on them.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    107[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    108[0m:[38;5;10m[49m Two `parse_command` cases (`op1_stop_unterminated`, `op1_resume_unterminated`)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    109[0m:[38;5;10m[49m do read residue and were nonetheless stable over 80 runs, because the bytes they
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    110[0m:[38;5;10m[49m touch fall in a zero-filled part of the start-up frame rather than on a saved
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    111[0m:[38;5;10m[49m pointer. They are kept as in-crate model tests in `src/main.rs`, not as
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    112[0m:[38;5;10m[49m differential assertions, so that an environment with a different start-up layout
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    113[0m:[38;5;10m[49m cannot turn the differential suite flaky.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    114[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    115[0m:[38;5;10m[49m ## Behaviour deliberately preserved (checked, no change needed)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    116[0m:[38;5;10m[49m 
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    117[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`size_t` underflow in `match_pattern`.** With `flags & 0x02` set and a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    118[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   pattern longer than the text, `for (size_t i = 0; i <= text_len - pattern_len; i++)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    119[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   underflows to a huge bound and `strncmp` walks off the end of the buffer. Both
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    120[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   binaries die with SIGSEGV (exit status 139 through a shell). Verified stable
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    121[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   over 110 text/pattern length combinations —
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    122[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `op4_match_pattern::op4_cs_pattern_longer_underflow*`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    123[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`strncpy` + `strncat` truncation in `compare_prefix`.** For a prefix of 63
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    124[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   bytes or more, `strncpy(expected, prefix, 63)` leaves no room, `strlen(expected)`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    125[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   is 63, and `strncat(..., 63 - 63)` appends nothing — so none of the `_v1`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    126[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `_v2`, `_old`, `_new`, `_tmp` variations can ever match. Covered at prefix
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    127[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   lengths 60/61/62/63/70 and at the 1024-byte maximum.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    128[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`snprintf` truncation to 63 bytes** for the three wildcard patterns in
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    129[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `match_pattern` — `op4_cs_long_pattern_wildcard_trunc`,
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    130[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `op4_cs_pattern_62_wild_both`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    131[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Flag masking.** Only bit 0 selects exact matching (op 2) and only bit 1
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    132[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   selects case sensitivity (op 4); every other bit, including
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    133[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `flags == 0xFFFFFFFF`, is ignored. Covered per operation.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    134[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`(char)byte` truncation** of each buffer byte read with `%u`: `321` stores
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    135[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `'A'`, `256` stores `0`, `-191` stores `'A'`. `operation` is likewise a
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    136[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `strtol` result truncated to `int`, and `flags` a `strtoul` result truncated to
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    137[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `unsigned int`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    138[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`find_delimiter` with a NUL delimiter** (`ref[0] == 0`): the `data[i] == delim`
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    139[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   test runs before the `data[i] == '\0'` break, so the function returns the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    140[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   index of the first NUL rather than `-1`.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    141[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`find_delimiter` scans `input_len` bytes, not the string.** A NUL before the
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    142[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   delimiter breaks the loop, and the `"NONE"` / `"EMPTY"` special cases are only
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    143[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reachable for delimiters `|` and `:` respectively.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    144[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **Order of the two length checks.** `input_len` is validated (and reported)
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    145[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   before `ref_len` is read at all, so a payload with both lengths out of range
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    146[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   reports only the input one.
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    147[0m:[38;5;10m[49m * **`process_strings`' `-1` and `-2` returns are unreachable** from this
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    148[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `main()`: `input_buffer` and `ref_buffer` are arrays, never `NULL`. The port
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    149[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   keeps the branches (modelled as `Option::None`) so the translation stays
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    150[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   faithful, but no stdin can reach them. The `-2` seen in test output comes from
[0m[49m[38;5;10m+    151[0m:[38;5;10m[49m   `find_delimiter`'s `"NONE"` special case, not from the NULL check.
